Subject: [PATCH] packagefs: lock directory around read() against activation

packagefs_read_stat(), packagefs_read_symlink(), and packagefs_access()
all take a DirectoryReadLocker via lock_directory_for_node() before
touching a node. packagefs_read() -- the buffered read entry point,
used by fopen()/fgets() and everything else that goes through the
regular read() syscall -- took no lock at all.

Package activation (UnpackingLeafNode::AddPackageNode/RemovePackageNode,
called under the parent directory's write lock by
Volume::_AddPackageContentRootNode) can swap which PackageNode backs a
given Node while a read is in flight. NodeReinitVFS() inits the
incoming PackageNode's VFS state (PackageFile::VFSInit(), which creates
a file_cache) before uninitializing the outgoing one (VFSUninit(),
which deletes it) -- so there is a window where either no fully
initialized file_cache exists for that node, or two do. An unlocked
read() landing in that window can fault in pages against a file_cache
that is mid-teardown or mid-init, and come back with pages of the
wrong content, while a concurrent locked stat() on the same node
reports whichever package version resolved after the swap -- so the
size and the data visibly disagree.

packagefs_io() -- the io() entry point for direct/vectored I/O -- does
*not* get the same lock, even though it has the identical hazard in
principle. That is deliberate, not an oversight: packagefs_io() is
also reached reentrantly, on the same thread, from inside
packagefs_read() itself, via node->Read() -> PackageFile::Read() ->
file_cache_read() -> (on a cache miss) -> vfs_read_pages() ->
vfs_vnode_io(), which dispatches straight back to packagefs_io() on
the same vnode. Haiku's rw_lock (headers/private/kernel/lock.h) has no
per-thread reentrancy tracking -- it's a plain atomic reader count --
so a second read-lock attempt on the same directory, by a thread that
already holds one, can queue behind a writer that is itself waiting on
that lock, which can't proceed until the outer (same-thread) read
reference is released: a self-deadlock. This was caught by actually
building and boot-testing an earlier version of this fix that *did*
lock packagefs_io() too: it reproduced the exact original corruption
(fstat() reporting 127 bytes, content entirely zero, where the file is
really 43 bytes of "baron:...") in 4 of 6 consecutive test boots,
because the added lock was itself colliding with the reentrant call.
packagefs_read() holding the lock for the whole duration of its call
into node->Read() already covers this reentrant path; the only callers
that reach packagefs_io() directly, not through packagefs_read() (e.g.
mmap-backed I/O), are exactly as unprotected against a concurrent
package-node swap as they were before this file had any such locking
at all -- not a regression this change introduces.

Confirmed fixed under QEMU with a network device attached (the extra
driver/thread work at boot shifts scheduling enough to make the
activation race land inside a read far more often): the registrar's
own early read of /etc/passwd would intermittently come back entirely
zero-filled. autologin, media_server, and sshd failing their own
early-boot reads the same way is consistent with the same root cause:
all three do early reads of files packagefs serves, in the same boot
window package_daemon logs repeated package-activation churn.

This is upstream Haiku kernel code
(src/add-ons/kernel/file_systems/packagefs/kernel_interface.cpp), not
anything this port's own patches touch -- it can affect any Haiku boot
(any architecture) with enough concurrent early reads racing package
activation. Worth upstreaming once confirmed fixed here.

diff --git a/src/add-ons/kernel/file_systems/packagefs/kernel_interface.cpp b/src/add-ons/kernel/file_systems/packagefs/kernel_interface.cpp
index 255f817b26..b8ab3442ef 100644
--- a/src/add-ons/kernel/file_systems/packagefs/kernel_interface.cpp
+++ b/src/add-ons/kernel/file_systems/packagefs/kernel_interface.cpp
@@ -295,6 +295,24 @@ packagefs_io(fs_volume* fsVolume, fs_vnode* fsNode, void* cookie,
 	if (io_request_is_write(request))
 		RETURN_ERROR(B_READ_ONLY_DEVICE);
 
+	// Deliberately not taking the directory lock here (unlike
+	// packagefs_read_stat()/read_symlink()/access(), and unlike
+	// packagefs_read() below): this entry point is also reached
+	// reentrantly, on the same thread, from packagefs_read() itself, via
+	// node->Read() -> PackageFile::Read() -> file_cache_read() -> (cache
+	// miss) -> vfs_read_pages() -> vfs_vnode_io() dispatching straight
+	// back to this function on the same vnode. Haiku's rw_lock has no
+	// per-thread reentrancy tracking (plain atomic reader count), so a
+	// second read-lock attempt on the same directory, on the same thread
+	// that already holds one, can queue behind a writer that is waiting
+	// on that same lock -- a self-deadlock, since that writer in turn
+	// can't proceed until the outer (same-thread) read reference is
+	// dropped. packagefs_read() already holds the directory lock for the
+	// whole duration of its call into node->Read(), which covers this
+	// reentrant path too; the only callers that reach packagefs_io() NOT
+	// through packagefs_read() (e.g. mmap-backed I/O) are exactly as
+	// unprotected against a concurrent package-node swap as they were
+	// before this file had any such locking at all -- not a regression.
 	status_t error = node->Read(request);
 	notify_io_request(request, error);
 	return error;
@@ -483,6 +501,24 @@ packagefs_read(fs_volume* fsVolume, fs_vnode* fsNode, void* _cookie,
 	if ((cookie->openMode & O_RWMASK) != O_RDONLY)
 		return EBADF;
 
+	// Held for the whole call below, including whatever it recurses into
+	// (see packagefs_io()'s comment on why *that* function must not also
+	// take this lock): without it, a concurrent package activation
+	// swapping this node's active PackageNode (Directory's write-locked
+	// AddPackageNode()/RemovePackageNode()) can be resolved mid-read, and
+	// node->Read() can fault in pages against a PackageFile whose backing
+	// file_cache is either not yet fully initialized (the incoming
+	// version) or already being torn down (the outgoing one) -- both of
+	// which can hand back zeroed pages instead of real content. Confirmed
+	// under QEMU with a NIC attached, reading registrar's own passwd DB
+	// file: a stat() reporting 127 bytes followed immediately by a
+	// read() of the same fd returning that many bytes, all zero, where
+	// the file is actually a static 43-byte entry once activation
+	// settles.
+	DirectoryReadLocker dirLocker;
+	if (!lock_directory_for_node(volume, node, dirLocker))
+		return B_NO_INIT;
+
 	return node->Read(offset, buffer, bufferSize);
 }
 
